# Grouped by what it is, because an incomplete rule here is expensive: these
# directories hold tens of GB that `git clean` will remove without asking.
# `results/` used to be the only output rule, which matched nothing, since real
# run directories are named results_star, results_hsc_salmon and so on.

# Nextflow state and run outputs
.nextflow/
.nextflow.log*
work/
results/
results*/
trace*.txt
timeline*.html
report*.html

# Reference data, indexes and downloaded reads. Large, re-fetchable, and never
# belonging in the repository. Both spellings of each: the docs use refs/ and
# idx/, older runs used references/ and indices/.
refs/
references/
idx/
indices/
test_data/
hsc_data/
*_data/

# Local run configs. conf/*.config is tracked; root-level ones are per-machine,
# e.g. star_sparse.config for a low-memory STAR build.
/*.config
!/nextflow.config

# Working notes, drafts and reviews. Not pipeline documentation.
working_docs/
future_improvements.md
to-fix.md

# Generated or tool-local
docs/
.claude/
__pycache__/
node_modules/
*.code-workspace
*.pptx
*.rar

# The deck's generated copies. scripts/slides/build_slides.js rebuilds both
# from rnaseq-flow.html, which is the authored source and is tracked, so there
# is nothing here that cannot be recreated in a few seconds.
/rnaseq-flow_Slides.pdf

# rnaseq-flow_User_Guide.docx is deliberately NOT ignored, although it is
# generated too. It is 59 kB and it is the only file someone can download on
# its own and read the whole guide from, which is worth a binary diff each time
# it is rebuilt. The deck's .pptx and .pdf are not: both rebuild from
# rnaseq-flow.html, which is tracked, in about fifteen seconds.
#
# Its generator is docs/build_user_guide.js, which this file ignores along with
# the rest of docs/, so the guide can only be rebuilt where that script is. The
# guide's prose lives in that script rather than being read from USAGE.md and
# OUTPUTS.md: it is a condensation of them, which is the point of it, and the
# script cites those two files as the long-form version. So editing either one
# does not change the guide. Keeping them in step is a manual job until the
# generator reads them.

# Scratch validators dropped at the repository root, which are per-machine.
# Anchored with a leading slash: unanchored, this pattern matches at any depth,
# and it was excluding tests/validate_topology.py from the repository
# altogether. The checker that verifies every process call's arity against its
# declared inputs was therefore not in any clone, which is how the four
# never-executed defects found on 2026-10-29 reached a release.
/validate*.py
